    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The search only covers seven days, so earlier misuse outside that window is not excluded. It is also not established that the burst was misuse rather than the owner acting.\n- **A2: Yes.** The burst happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes, so the proxy, the new fingerprint and the payout changes already existed when it scored. This assumes the check could see request and account-change data, which the case does not state.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination fits one owner, but it also fits a shared processor or a fraudster reusing one destination.\n- **A4: Yes, weakly.** Thirty days is long enough that a diverted payout would often be noticed, so silence is some evidence, but it is weak because reports can lag or never be filed.\n- **A5: Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout changes, is a strong enough signal to investigate.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: Yes.** D has 0 chargebacks in 100 transactions against 2 in 100 for each of the others, so its recorded rate is lower (though the difference is small).\n- **B2: No.** A combined 5 percent says nothing about each account; one can be above it while another is below.\n- **B3: Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. This holds for accounts recorded under that person's identity; it would not catch accounts held through nominees.\n- **B4: Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; they may have used the same evidence, or the second may have read the first note.\n- **B5: No.** The payment was blocked, so it could never produce a chargeback; the missing chargeback says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates, a common descriptor pattern and settlement through the same four accounts are enough to justify looking at them together.\n\nRESULT CF-034 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Ye